    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_14c882c3a8ee45bc7cd9d4da1cd0364623e703e382de62969d0a6990039f6aa4",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Conrey's parameterized mollified mean square is a common variational interface, but two different auxiliary constructions determine what defect it measures. For derivative zeros, Littlewood and Jensen turn zeros of J_k on or left of the critical line into an L2 cost; integration by parts replaces J_k by V_k with Q_k(x)=(1-x)^k, and optimizing the mollifier makes the cost approach 2, yielding a right-side derivative-zero density approaching one as k grows. For distinct zeta zeros, the xi-plus-xi' auxiliary f deletes simple zeros and reduces each multiple-zero multiplicity by one; symmetry, Littlewood, and Jensen turn that excess multiplicity into the same kind of L2 cost, now with an admissible linear Q satisfying Q(0)=1, whose imported optimization gives kappa_d>0.70.",
