    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_94b8e7a3cdeb7c623563759dd4820c6ab71748336a0f6a98a4f45847280fbea7",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "High-strip asymptotics and nonvanishing for the chi derivatives make it possible to divide the differentiated functional equation by chi^(m) and define J_m without introducing functional-equation-factor zeros. Zeros of J_m then encode reflected zeros of zeta^(m), so Littlewood's lemma converts their leftward displacement into a boundary integral while explicitly retaining a rightward zeta-zero displacement term. Fractional-moment control of successive logarithmic derivatives bounds that boundary integral, producing the comparison theorem; Selberg's estimate, functional-equation symmetry, and the cited signed total-displacement formula then respectively bound the left derivative-zero mass and isolate the right-side main term.",
